The '''second series''' of ''[[The Great British Bake Off]]'' started 16 August 2011 with twelve amateur bakers.  Unlike series one, the competition was held in a single location – the grounds of [[Valentines Mansion]], a 17th-century mansion house in [[London Borough of Redbridge|Redbridge]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www2.redbridge.gov.uk/cms/news_and_events/latest_news/2011/august_2011/valentines_mansion_proves_to_b.aspx |title=Valentines Mansion proves to be the perfect setting for TV show |date=16 August 2011 |work=Redbridge i |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120106183612/http://www2.redbridge.gov.uk/cms/news_and_events/latest_news/2011/august_2011/valentines_mansion_proves_to_b.aspx |archivedate=6 January 2012 }}</ref> In addition to their on-screen presenting, Mel Giedroyc and Sue Perkins also took over the narration, which had been done by Stephen Noonan for the previous series. Series two also introduced the "star baker" award for the most impressive performer each week.

Three and a half thousand people applied for the competition, and twelve were selected. Each episode was filmed over two fourteen-hour days.<ref name="metro">{{cite web |url=http://metro.co.uk/2012/08/28/edd-kimber-and-jo-wheatley-winning-the-great-british-bake-off-was-life-changing-554519/ |title= Edd Kimber and Jo Wheatley: Great British Bake Off changed our lives
|date=28 August 2012 |work=Metro }}</ref>{{unreliable source|sure=yes|reason=Per WP:METRO|date=July 2025}} The competition was won by [[Jo Wheatley]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Joanne Wheatley recipes – BBC Food|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/food/chefs/joanne_wheatley|website=www.bbc.co.uk|access-date=2020-05-19}}</ref>

